92 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 75 0 R
/T (In this project we tried to predict whether a signal detected by the Kepler space telescope is a real planet candidate or a false positive. We used only the physical properties of the transit and the host star. Our best model was XGBoost, which got 87.0% accuracy and a ROC-AUC of 0.93 on a test set of stars it had never seen. Our baseline, logistic regression, got 80.8%, so the improvement was about 6 percentage points.)
/E (In this project we tried to predict whether a signal detected by the Kepler space telescope is a real planet candidate or a false positive. We used only the physical properties of the transit and the host star. Our best model was XGBoost, which got 87.0% accuracy and a ROC-AUC of 0.93 on a test set of stars it had never seen. Our baseline, logistic regression, got 80.8%, so the improvement was about 6 percentage points.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
93 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 75 0 R
/T (The features that mattered most were the planet radius, the number of other signals found on the same star, and the orbital period. When we looked at the mistakes, most of them turned out to be background eclipsing binaries. These look just like small planets in our data, and we think they can only be caught using pixel-level data from the telescope.)
/E (The features that mattered most were the planet radius, the number of other signals found on the same star, and the orbital period. When we looked at the mistakes, most of them turned out to be background eclipsing binaries. These look just like small planets in our data, and we think they can only be caught using pixel-level data from the telescope.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
