96 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 75 0 R
/T (The Kepler telescope watched about 150,000 stars and looked for small, regular dips in their brightness. A dip like this can mean a planet is passing in front of the star. But a lot of these signals are not planets at all. They can be two stars eclipsing each other, light from a background star mixing in, or just noise from the instrument. Each signal is called a Kepler Object of Interest \(KOI\), and Kepler's pipeline labels each one as either a CANDIDATE or a FALSE POSITIVE.)
/E (The Kepler telescope watched about 150,000 stars and looked for small, regular dips in their brightness. A dip like this can mean a planet is passing in front of the star. But a lot of these signals are not planets at all. They can be two stars eclipsing each other, light from a background star mixing in, or just noise from the instrument. Each signal is called a Kepler Object of Interest \(KOI\), and Kepler's pipeline labels each one as either a CANDIDATE or a FALSE POSITIVE.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
