136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 77 0 R
/T (At first we thought about removing these, but we decided to keep them. They are not errors in the data. For example, a "planet" with a radius of 50 Earth radii, or a transit that blocks 10% of the star's light, is almost always another star. These extreme values are exactly what helps tell the classes apart, so removing them would throw away the easiest false positives and make our results look better than they really are. Instead, we log- transformed the skewed features for logistic regression. Tree-based models are not affected by outliers in the same way, so they did not need this.)
/E (At first we thought about removing these, but we decided to keep them. They are not errors in the data. For example, a "planet" with a radius of 50 Earth radii, or a transit that blocks 10% of the star's light, is almost always another star. These extreme values are exactly what helps tell the classes apart, so removing them would throw away the easiest false positives and make our results look better than they really are. Instead, we log- transformed the skewed features for logistic regression. Tree-based models are not affected by outliers in the same way, so they did not need this.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
