290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 85 0 R
/T (Our new features helped, but only a little: about 0.004 in AUC and 0.7 percentage points in accuracy. Splitting by star also made very little difference here. This is because 89% of stars have only one KOI, so there was not much leakage to begin with. We still think splitting by star is the right approach, because it makes sure our results are not inflated.)
/E (Our new features helped, but only a little: about 0.004 in AUC and 0.7 percentage points in accuracy. Splitting by star also made very little difference here. This is because 89% of stars have only one KOI, so there was not much leakage to begin with. We still think splitting by star is the right approach, because it makes sure our results are not inflated.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 85 0 R
/T (The 120 false positives that the model called candidates look almost the same as real small planets. Their median radius was 1.6 Earth radii, and their SNR and period were close to those of the real candidates. They are very different from the obvious false positives, the giant star-sized ones, which the model catches easily.)
/E (The 120 false positives that the model called candidates look almost the same as real small planets. Their median radius was 1.6 Earth radii, and their SNR and period were close to those of the real candidates. They are very different from the obvious false positives, the giant star-sized ones, which the model catches easily.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
292 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 85 0 R
/T (To find out what these signals actually were, we looked at the archive's false positive flags. We did not use these flags for training, only for this analysis. 71% of the missed false positives had the centroid offset flag \(koi_fpflag_co\), compared with 40% of the false positives the model got right. A centroid offset means the dip is actually coming from a nearby background star whose light is blended in. Our features cannot detect this, since you need the telescope's pixel data to see where the light is coming from.)
/E (To find out what these signals actually were, we looked at the archive's false positive flags. We did not use these flags for training, only for this analysis. 71% of the missed false positives had the centroid offset flag \(koi_fpflag_co\), compared with 40% of the false positives the model got right. A centroid offset means the dip is actually coming from a nearby background star whose light is blended in. Our features cannot detect this, since you need the telescope's pixel data to see where the light is coming from.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
293 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 85 0 R
/T (The 91 candidates that the model rejected tended to have shorter periods \(median 3.3 days\), bigger radii \(3.4 Earth radii\) and more grazing transits \(impact 0.64\). These values overlap with what eclipsing binaries look like, which is probably why the model got confused.)
/E (The 91 candidates that the model rejected tended to have shorter periods \(median 3.3 days\), bigger radii \(3.4 Earth radii\) and more grazing transits \(impact 0.64\). These values overlap with what eclipsing binaries look like, which is probably why the model got confused.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
