327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 86 0 R
/T (We also found that some of these "mistakes" might not really be mistakes. Out of the 91 rejected candidates, 77 are still unconfirmed in the final archive. And out of the 120 false positives that the model called candidates, 7 were later confirmed as real planets, so in those cases the model was right and the original label was wrong.)
/E (We also found that some of these "mistakes" might not really be mistakes. Out of the 91 rejected candidates, 77 are still unconfirmed in the final archive. And out of the 120 false positives that the model called candidates, 7 were later confirmed as real planets, so in those cases the model was right and the original label was wrong.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 86 0 R
/T (Kepler finds some planets much more easily than others, mainly large planets with short orbits around bright stars similar to the Sun. This means our model has not seen many small planets with long orbits, or planets around very cool or very large stars, and it is probably less reliable for them.)
/E (Kepler finds some planets much more easily than others, mainly large planets with short orbits around bright stars similar to the Sun. This means our model has not seen many small planets with long orbits, or planets around very cool or very large stars, and it is probably less reliable for them.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 86 0 R
/T (Our n_koi_on_star feature also has a bias. It favours stars where several signals were found. If a star has a real planet but its other planets were missed, the model is more likely to reject it.)
/E (Our n_koi_on_star feature also has a bias. It favours stars where several signals were found. If a star has a real planet but its other planets were missed, the model is more likely to reject it.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 86 0 R
/T (The model is also specific to Kepler. Another telescope like TESS has different noise and resolution, so the model would need to be retrained to work there.)
/E (The model is also specific to Kepler. Another telescope like TESS has different noise and resolution, so the model would need to be retrained to work there.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 86 0 R
/T (The biggest limitation is that our target is the label given by Kepler's automated pipeline, and that label is not always correct. So our model is really learning to copy the pipeline's decisions, including its mistakes. Only 2,731 of the KOIs have actually been confirmed as planets.)
/E (The biggest limitation is that our target is the label given by Kepler's automated pipeline, and that label is not always correct. So our model is really learning to copy the pipeline's decisions, including its mistakes. Only 2,731 of the KOIs have actually been confirmed as planets.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
332 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 86 0 R
/T (Some of our features, such as planet radius and impact parameter, come from fitting a planet model to the transit. For signals that are really binary stars, these values are not physically correct, although they still turned out to be useful for the model.)
/E (Some of our features, such as planet radius and impact parameter, come from fitting a planet model to the transit. For signals that are really binary stars, these values are not physically correct, although they still turned out to be useful for the model.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 86 0 R
/T (We also did not use any pixel-level or light curve data. As the error analysis showed, this is what limits our accuracy the most, since it is needed to catch blended background stars. Finally, filling the 59 incomplete rows with the median is a simple approach. These rows could be different from the rest in some way, but there are too few of them to change our results much.)
/E (We also did not use any pixel-level or light curve data. As the error analysis showed, this is what limits our accuracy the most, since it is needed to catch blended background stars. Finally, filling the 59 incomplete rows with the median is a simple approach. These rows could be different from the rest in some way, but there are too few of them to change our results much.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
