832 0 obj
<<
/Title (We used the IQR rule to count outliers in each feature. Some features had a lot of them:)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 568 0 R
/Next 950 0 R
>>
endobj
833 0 obj
<<
/Title (At first we thought about removing these, but we decided to keep them. They are not errors in the data. For example, a "planet" with a radius of 50 Earth radii, or a transit that blocks 10% of the star's light, is almost always another star. These extreme values are exactly what helps tell the classes apart, so removing them would throw away the easiest false positives and make our results look better than they really are. Instead, we log- transformed the skewed features for logistic regression. Tree-based models are not affected by outliers in the same way, so they did not need this.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 568 0 R
/Prev 951 0 R
>>
endobj
834 0 obj
<<
/Title (All the size-related features were very right-skewed, so we plotted them on log scales to compare the two classes. A few things stood out:)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 569 0 R
/Next 952 0 R
>>
endobj
835 0 obj
<<
