<<
/Title (V. Sarayu Anushree Chandra)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 369 0 R
/Prev 825 0 R
/Next 942 0 R
>>
endobj
942 0 obj
<<
/Title (Roll number EC24B1106 EC24B1110 EC24B1119)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 369 0 R
/Prev 941 0 R
/Next 826 0 R
>>
endobj
943 0 obj
<<
/Title (In this project we tried to predict whether a signal detected by the Kepler space telescope is a real planet candidate or a false positive. We used only the physical properties of the transit and the host star. Our best model was XGBoost, which got 87.0% accuracy and a ROC-AUC of 0.93 on a test set of stars it had never seen. Our baseline, logistic regression, got 80.8%, so the improvement was about 6 percentage points.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 826 0 R
/Next 944 0 R
>>
endobj
944 0 obj
<<
/Title (The features that mattered most were the planet radius, the number of other signals found on the same star, and the orbital period. When we looked at the mistakes, most of them turned out to be background eclipsing binaries. These look just like small planets in our data, and we think they can only be caught using pixel-level data from the telescope.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 826 0 R
/Prev 943 0 R
>>
endobj
945 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Kepler telescope watched about 150,000 stars and looked for small, regular dips in their brightness. A dip like this can mean a planet is passing in front of the star. But a lot of these signals are not planets at all. They can be two stars eclipsing each other, light from a background star mixing in, or just noise from the instrument. Each signal is called a Kepler Object of Interest \(KOI\), and Kepler's pipeline labels each one as either a CANDIDATE or a FALSE POSITIVE.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 827 0 R
/Next 946 0 R
>>
endobj
946 0 obj
<<
/Title (Our question was: can we predict this label using only the physical measurements of the transit and the star? If this works, it could help astronomers decide which signals to follow up first, since telescope time for confirming planets is limited.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 827 0 R
/Prev 945 0 R
>>
endobj
947 0 obj
<<
/Title (2. Get at least 85% accuracy on the test set without using any column that comes from)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 370 0 R
/Prev 828 0 R
/Next 1059 0 R
>>
endobj
948 0 obj
<<
/Title (6,923 \(775 of them have more than one KOI\) 4,034 CANDIDATE, 4,020 FALSE POSITIVE)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 370 0 R
/Prev 1060 0 R
/Next 829 0 R
>>
endobj
949 0 obj
<<
/Title (There were no duplicate rows. For missing values, we noticed that the same 59 rows \(about 0.7% of the data\) were missing six of our features at once: depth, SNR, impact, planet radius, star temperature and star radius. Period and duration had no missing values. Since it was only a small group of rows, we filled the gaps with the median. We made sure the median was calculated only from the training data, by doing the filling inside a scikit- learn Pipeline. XGBoost can deal with missing values on its own, so we left them as they were for that model. All our features were already numeric, and we converted the target to 1 for CANDIDATE and 0 for FALSE POSITIVE.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 831 0 R
>>
endobj
950 0 obj
<<
/Title (Feature koi_depth koi_model_snr koi_period koi_prad koi_srad koi_duration koi_steff koi_impact)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 568 0 R
/Prev 832 0 R
/Next 1061 0 R
>>
endobj
