1071 0 obj
<<
/Title (The 120 false positives that the model called candidates look almost the same as real small planets. Their median radius was 1.6 Earth radii, and their SNR and period were close to those of the real candidates. They are very different from the obvious false positives, the giant star-sized ones, which the model catches easily.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 837 0 R
/Prev 957 0 R
/Next 1142 0 R
>>
endobj
1072 0 obj
<<
/Title (The 91 candidates that the model rejected tended to have shorter periods \(median 3.3 days\), bigger radii \(3.4 Earth radii\) and more grazing transits \(impact 0.64\). These values overlap with what eclipsing binaries look like, which is probably why the model got confused.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 837 0 R
/Prev 1142 0 R
/Next 958 0 R
>>
endobj
1073 0 obj
<<
/Title (The biggest limitation is that our target is the label given by Kepler's automated pipeline, and that label is not always correct. So our model is really learning to copy the pipeline's decisions, including its mistakes. Only 2,731 of the KOIs have actually been confirmed as planets.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 839 0 R
/Prev 960 0 R
/Next 1143 0 R
>>
endobj
1074 0 obj
<<
/Title (Our n_koi_on_star feature also has a bias. It favours stars where several signals were found. If a star has a real planet but its other planets were missed, the model is more likely to reject it.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 839 0 R
/Prev 1144 0 R
/Next 961 0 R
>>
endobj
1075 0 obj
<<
/Title (1. Add diagnostic features that help catch blended binaries, like centroid offset)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 961 0 R
/Next 1145 0 R
>>
endobj
