1142 0 obj
<<
/Title (To find out what these signals actually were, we looked at the archive's false positive flags. We did not use these flags for training, only for this analysis. 71% of the missed false positives had the centroid offset flag \(koi_fpflag_co\), compared with 40% of the false positives the model got right. A centroid offset means the dip is actually coming from a nearby background star whose light is blended in. Our features cannot detect this, since you need the telescope's pixel data to see where the light is coming from.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 837 0 R
/Prev 1071 0 R
/Next 1072 0 R
>>
endobj
1143 0 obj
<<
/Title (Some of our features, such as planet radius and impact parameter, come from fitting a planet model to the transit. For signals that are really binary stars, these values are not physically correct, although they still turned out to be useful for the model.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 839 0 R
/Prev 1073 0 R
/Next 1164 0 R
>>
endobj
1144 0 obj
<<
/Title (Kepler finds some planets much more easily than others, mainly large planets with short orbits around bright stars similar to the Sun. This means our model has not seen many small planets with long orbits, or planets around very cool or very large stars, and it is probably less reliable for them.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 839 0 R
/Prev 1164 0 R
/Next 1074 0 R
>>
endobj
