1190 0 obj
<<
/Title (One thing we noticed was that some stars have more than one KOI, and these KOIs share the same star properties. If we split the rows randomly, the model could see one planet of a star during training and then be tested on another planet of the same star, which is a kind of leakage. So we split the data by star instead, using GroupShuffleSplit on kepid. This gave 6,433 KOIs for training and 1,621 for testing, with no star appearing in both.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1171 0 R
/Next 1191 0 R
>>
endobj
1191 0 obj
<<
/Title (On the training set, we used 5-fold StratifiedGroupKFold cross-validation, which also keeps each star in a single fold. We used it both to tune hyperparameters and to choose the final model. We only used the test set once, at the very end. All the preprocessing steps \(filling missing values, log transform and scaling\) were put inside Pipelines so that they were fitted on the training folds only. We set the random seed to 42 everywhere so the results can be reproduced.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1171 0 R
/Prev 1190 0 R
/First 1210 0 R
/Last 1211 0 R
/Count -11
>>
endobj
1192 0 obj
<<
/Title (Since the two classes are balanced, we report accuracy. We also report the F1 score for the CANDIDATE class and ROC-AUC. We think ROC-AUC is the most useful one for this problem, because in practice astronomers would use the model to rank signals by how likely they are to be real, and ROC-AUC measures how good that ranking is.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1172 0 R
>>
endobj
